{{also|eloqüent|éloquent}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|fro|eloquent}}, from {{der|en|la|eloquens||speaking, having the faculty of speech, eloquent}}, present participle of {{m|la|eloquor||to speak out}}, from {{m|la|e||out}} + {{m|la|loquor||to speak}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɛl.əˌkwənt/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-I learned some phrases-eloquent.wav|a=UK}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[fluently|Fluently]] [[persuasive]] and [[articulate]].
#: {{syn|en|articulate|well-spoken}}
#: {{ux|en|an '''eloquent''' writer}}
# [[effective|Effective]] in [[expressing]] [[meaning]] by [[speech]].
#: {{ux|en|an '''eloquent''' article}}
# {{lb|en|medicine}} Relating to [[area]]s in the [[brain]] that [[serve]] an [[identifiable]] [[neurological]] [[function]], in which [[injury]] leads to [[focal]] [[deficit]]s or [[disability]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2016-02-07|author=Caroline Davies|title=Been anywhere nice this year? Brain surgery where patients are kept chatting|journal=w:The Guardian|url=https://www.theguardian.com/society/2016/feb/07/been-anywhere-nice-this-year-brain-surgery-where-patients-are-kept-chatting|passage=Lydiard was diagnosed shortly after new year following speech disturbance and subtle memory issues. He has a high-grade glioma, an aggressive malignant primary brain tumour in the left side. It is in the “'''eloquent''' brain”, very close to the part that controls speech, and would have been considered untreatable in the past.}}

====Usage notes====
''Eloquent'' expresses stronger praise than do {{m|en|articulate}} or {{m|en|well-spoken}}.

